Analyze the following vulnerability patch and generate a precise Semgrep rule specification (as a JSON object) to detect similar vulnerabilities in java code.

    CONTEXT:
    Repository: github.com/jenkinsci/git-client-plugin
    Commit: 75ea3fe05650fc6ca09046a72493e2b3f066fb98

    PATCH CHANGES:
    File: src/main/java/org/jenkinsci/plugins/gitclient/CliGitAPIImpl.java
--- a/src/main/java/org/jenkinsci/plugins/gitclient/CliGitAPIImpl.java
+++ b/src/main/java/org/jenkinsci/plugins/gitclient/CliGitAPIImpl.java
+import java.nio.file.Path;
+import java.nio.file.Paths;
+import java.nio.file.attribute.FileAttribute;
+import java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermission;
+import java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermissions;
+    private File createTempFileInSystemDir(String prefix, String suffix) throws IOException {
+        if (isWindows()) {
+            return Files.createTempFile(prefix, suffix).toFile();
+        }
+        Set<PosixFilePermission> ownerOnly = PosixFilePermissions.fromString("rw-------");
+        FileAttribute fileAttribute = PosixFilePermissions.asFileAttribute(ownerOnly);
+        return Files.createTempFile(prefix, suffix, fileAttribute).toFile();
+    }
+
+    private File createTempFile(String prefix, String suffix) throws IOException {
+        if (workspace == null) {
+            return createTempFileInSystemDir(prefix, suffix);
+        }
+        File workspaceTmp = new File(workspace.getAbsolutePath() + "@tmp");
+        if (!workspaceTmp.isDirectory() && !workspaceTmp.mkdirs()) {
+            if (!workspaceTmp.isDirectory()) {
+                return createTempFileInSystemDir(prefix, suffix);
+            }
+        }
+        Path tmpPath = Paths.get(workspaceTmp.getAbsolutePath());
+        if (isWindows()) {
+            return Files.createTempFile(tmpPath, prefix, suffix).toFile();
+        }
+        Set<PosixFilePermission> ownerOnly = PosixFilePermissions.fromString("rw-------");
+        FileAttribute fileAttribute = PosixFilePermissions.asFileAttribute(ownerOnly);
+        return Files.createTempFile(tmpPath, prefix, suffix, fileAttribute).toFile();
+    }
+
-            msg = File.createTempFile("git-note", "txt", workspace);
+            msg = createTempFile("git-note", ".txt");
-        File key = File.createTempFile("ssh", "key");
+        File key = createTempFile("ssh", ".key");
-        File ssh = File.createTempFile("pass", ".bat");
+        File ssh = createTempFile("pass", ".bat");
-        File ssh = File.createTempFile("pass", ".sh");
+        File ssh = createTempFile("pass", ".sh");
-        File askpass = File.createTempFile("pass", ".bat");
+        File askpass = createTempFile("pass", ".bat");
-        File askpass = File.createTempFile("pass", ".sh");
+        File askpass = createTempFile("pass", ".sh");
-        File ssh = File.createTempFile("ssh", ".bat");
+        File ssh = createTempFile("ssh", ".bat");
-        File ssh = File.createTempFile("ssh", ".sh");
+        File ssh = createTempFile("ssh", ".sh");
-            f = File.createTempFile("gitcommit", ".txt");
+            f = createTempFile("gitcommit", ".txt");

    JSON FIELDS (emit a single JSON object with exactly these field names):
    - "id": "vuln-git-client-plugin-75ea3fe0" (must be unique and descriptive, lowercase letters/digits/hyphens only)
    - "message": detailed description of what the vulnerability is, why it's dangerous, and how to fix it
    - "severity": one of "ERROR", "WARNING", "INFO"
    - "languages": ["java"]
    - "metadata": an object, e.g. {"category": "security", "cwe": "...", "owasp": "..."}
    - exactly ONE of:
        "pattern": a single pattern string matching the vulnerable code structure, using $VARNAME for
            any expression and ... for any sequence of statements
        "pattern_either": a list of alternative pattern strings (any of which should match)
        "taint_sources" + "taint_sinks": each a list of pattern strings (taint-tracking mode)
    - optionally also:
        "pattern_not": a list of one or more pattern strings that should NOT match (e.g. the fixed
            version) -- you do not need to know how this combines with "pattern"; that is handled
            for you
        "pattern_inside": a single pattern string restricting matches to inside this context
        "pattern_not_inside": a single pattern string excluding matches inside this context

    IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
    1. Make "pattern" (or "pattern_either") as specific as possible to minimize false positives
    2. Include "pattern_not" for the fixed version when possible
    3. Add relevant metadata like CWE numbers and OWASP categories
    4. Write a clear, actionable "message" explaining both the problem and the fix
    5. Consider different variations of the vulnerable pattern

    EXAMPLES OF HIGH-QUALITY RULE SPECIFICATIONS:
    {
  "id": "path-traversal",
  "pattern": "new File($PATH)",
  "pattern_not": [
    "new File(sanitized_path)"
  ],
  "pattern_inside": "class $CLASS {\n    ...\n}",
  "languages": [
    "java"
  ],
  "message": "Potential path traversal vulnerability. Validate and sanitize file paths.",
  "severity": "ERROR",
  "metadata": {
    "category": "security",
    "cwe": "CWE-22"
  }
}

{
  "id": "weak-cipher",
  "pattern": "Cipher.getInstance(\"DES\")",
  "languages": [
    "java"
  ],
  "message": "Usage of weak cryptographic algorithm DES detected. Use AES instead.",
  "severity": "ERROR",
  "metadata": {
    "category": "security",
    "cwe": "CWE-326"
  }
}

    Respond with ONLY the JSON object, nothing else.

